<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Release entitlements.

  It was an empty dict until CloudKit, and the emptiness was itself a
  finding: the hardened runtime restricts JIT, unsigned executable memory,
  DYLD injection and loading libraries this app did not sign — not
  spawning child processes, which is the terminal's whole job. libghostty
  asked for none of it. Everything below is here because CloudKit needs
  it, and nothing else was added along for the ride.

  NOT app-sandbox. Synapty spawns arbitrary child processes, SSHes out and
  drives tmux; sandboxing it would not be a hardening step, it would be a
  different product. If CloudKit turns out to require the sandbox on
  macOS, that is a dead end to discover in the first ten minutes rather
  than after a sync engine is written — see SyncPreflight.

  These are RESTRICTED entitlements: they are only valid when the embedded
  provisioning profile authorises them, which is why Release now embeds
  one and Debug does not need to.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Xcode normally injects these two, and
	     CODE_SIGN_INJECT_BASE_ENTITLEMENTS=NO — which is how
	     get-task-allow is kept out — removes them along with it. That is
	     not cosmetic: CloudKit identifies the app by
	     application-identifier, and without it CKContainer fails at
	     construction with "Trying to initialize a container without an
	     application ID". The build signs, notarizes, launches and reports
	     itself entitled, and sync never starts. Caught by running the
	     shipped build rather than by reading the signature. -->
	<key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
	<string>HSEZTB9EQB.com.synapty.app</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
	<string>HSEZTB9EQB</string>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-services</key>
	<array>
		<string>CloudKit</string>
	</array>
	<key>com.apple.developer.icloud-container-identifiers</key>
	<array>
		<string>iCloud.com.synapty.app</string>
	</array>
	<!-- CKSyncEngine learns about remote changes through silent pushes.
	     Without this the data still converges, but only when the app
	     happens to fetch — a change on one Mac would not appear on
	     another until something else prompted a sync. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.aps-environment</key>
	<string>production</string>
</dict>
</plist>
